<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.1.4: The Input and Output of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01746</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.1.4: The Input and Output of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3766ff46-9e18-48ec-ac0d-8a44074cef70</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A <emphasis>rectangular</emphasis>yard is enclosed by 100 meters of fencing. The table shows some possible values for the length and width of the yard.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="3">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>Length (meters)</emphasis></entry>
              <entry><emphasis>Width (meters)</emphasis></entry>
              <entry><emphasis>Area (square meters)</emphasis></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>10</entry>
              <entry>40</entry>
              <entry>400</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>20</entry>
              <entry>30</entry>
              <entry> </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>25</entry>
              <entry>25</entry>
              <entry>625</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>35</entry>
              <entry>15</entry>
              <entry>525</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>40</entry>
              <entry> </entry>
              <entry> </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Complete the table with the missing values.</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00002">
            <tgroup cols="3">
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry><emphasis>Length (meters)</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Width (meters)</emphasis></entry>
                  <entry><emphasis>Area (square meters)</emphasis></entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                  <entry>40</entry>
                  <entry>400</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>20</entry>
                  <entry>30</entry>
                  <entry>600</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>25</entry>
                  <entry>25</entry>
                  <entry>625</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>35</entry>
                  <entry>15</entry>
                  <entry>525</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>40</entry>
                  <entry>10</entry>
                  <entry>400</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </tgroup>
          </table>
          <para id="para-00005">The missing value in the second row is 600. The missing values in the last row are 10 and 400.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">If the values for length and area are plotted, what would the graph look like?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">The points would form a stretched out upside-down U shape, or an arch.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">How is the relationship between the length and the area of the rectangle different from other kinds of relationships we’ve seen before?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">As one quantity increases, the other quantity first increases and then decreases, instead of always increasing or always decreasing.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
